WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.840
Three squared is nine.

2
00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:04.230
Take away one, and you get eight.

3
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.410
Five squared, minus one, is twenty-four.

4
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:08.990
That's three eights.

5
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.690
Seven squared, minus one?

6
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.320
Also a multiple of eight.

7
00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.020
Nine squared, minus one?

8
00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.100
Eight goes into that exactly, too.

9
00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.700
But does that settle
it for all odd numbers,

10
00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.030
even ones far too big to check?

11
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.140
In this mission, you'll see why
checking examples is not a proof.

12
00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.000
The next number might fail.

13
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.250
You'll write any odd number with algebra,

14
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.670
as two n plus one, where n is an integer.

15
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.930
Then you'll expand and factorise, so a hidden
factor steps out in front of a bracket.

16
00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:43.260
Your mission: decide whether squaring
any odd number and taking away one always

17
00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:44.670
gives a multiple of eight.
